A||||13||Fiero_24||This stinks!!!!!!  Are Fiero's Forgotten?||||8||
Z||000000||Fiero_24||07-22-2001||01:11 PM||||I just sat through a 1 hour documentary on the 2 seater sports cars on the history channel.  I was hoping to get a glimpse of a Fiero (fat chance).  To make a long story short, they showed a Mazda Miata and no Fiero's.  Fiero's are Americas first production mid engine car (We all know this), and they can show a Miata and no Fiero?  What a bunch of crap.  Yes they did leave out the MR2 and the CRX, but this is crap that they could leave out and American made car in limited production.  Does anyone else feel that they just pretend the car never existed?  It feels good to have a car no one really knows about, but a little recognition feels good too.  At the dealership yesterday one of the sales men pulled up in a old MR2 next to my 87 GT.  He was mad that more people were asking the price of the Fiero over the MR2 (too bad the Fiero was not for sale).  At least it was a little recognition and not the usual, "Didn't those car use to blow up?".<P>Craig<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||8
Z||000001||Mach10||07-22-2001||01:20 PM||||Actually, wasn't the Corvair the first production mid-engine? I think the Fiero was the only production 2-seater mid-engine...<BR>Actually, GM had classified the Fiero as an economy car, not a sports car. That may have had something to do with it...||||reg||7
